            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Turns class numbers into one-hot vectors: class 2 of 4 becomes 0 0 1 0 - a 1 in the class's place and 0 everywhere else.\n\nIt is the form most networks take a choice in, and the form their training targets are written in: a note from a scale, a gesture from a vocabulary, a performer from a cast, given to t.model as a vector rather than a number (a number would suggest that class 3 is \"more\" than class 1). Fed back, the choices t.multinomial makes become the network's next input this way. A list of class numbers gives one row each.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.one_hot <classes>\nDefault: classes -1 - as many as the largest class number needs.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nindices:\nA class number, or a list or tensor of them: whole numbers from 0. Every input is converted and sent.\n\nclasses (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many classes there are - the length of each vector. Give it, so a vector has the same length whatever classes happen to arrive; -1 takes the largest class number plus one. A class number at or past it is refused.\n\noutput_type:\nAn option, under the node: float (the default, what networks take), int or bool.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\none hot:\nOne vector per class number: a single number gives one vector, a list gives one row each.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: the class numbers 2 0 3 1, with 4 classes: four rows, each with its 1 in a different place. Bottom: the single class 2 gives 0 0 1 0 - as one row, because a message box sends 2 as a list of one. A plain number (from an int box) gives the vector itself.\n\nRELATED:\nt.argmax goes the other way, from a vector of scores back to a class number. t.multinomial picks a class number by probability. t.model takes the vectors.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Turns class numbers into one-hot vectors: class 2 of 4 becomes 0 0 1 0 - a 1 in the class's place and 0 everywhere else.\n\nIt is the form most networks take a choice in, and the form their training targets are written in: a note from a scale, a gesture from a vocabulary, a performer from a cast, given to t.model as a vector rather than a number (a number would suggest that class 3 is \"more\" than class 1). Fed back, the choices t.multinomial makes become the network's next input this way. A list of class numbers gives one row each.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.one_hot <classes>\nDefault: classes -1 - as many as the largest class number needs.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nindices:\nA class number, or a list or tensor of them: whole numbers from 0. Every input is converted and sent.\n\nclasses (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many classes there are - the length of each vector. Give it, so a vector has the same length whatever classes happen to arrive; -1 takes the largest class number plus one. A class number at or past it is refused.\n\noutput_type:\nAn option, under the node: float (the default, what networks take), int or bool.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\none hot:\nOne vector per class number: a single number gives one vector, a list gives one row each.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: the class numbers 2 0 3 1, with 4 classes: four rows, each with its 1 in a different place. Bottom: the single class 2 gives 0 0 1 0 - as one row, because a message box sends 2 as a list of one. A plain number (from an int box) gives the vector itself.\n\nRELATED:\nt.argmax goes the other way, from a vector of scores back to a class number. t.multinomial picks a class number by probability. t.model takes the vectors."
        },
